{
  "$comment": "Cross-language conformance fixture for Postman `:name` path variables (issue #1764). Read by BOTH the engine gtest suite (path_variables_test.cpp, via VAYU_ENGINE_SOURCE_DIR) and the app's vitest suite (app/src/modules/request-builder/utils/path-variables.conformance.test.ts). The engine composes the request (`core::path_variable_segments`, `core::substitute_path_variables`); the app draws the Params tab's Sends line and the code snippets from its own copy (`pathVariableSegments`, `substitutePathVariables`), so a case answered two ways is a request that shows one URL and sends another. Each case: `url`; `rows`, a request's Params rows; `segments`, every `:name` segment of `url` with the offset of its colon and the length of `:name` (a `.suffix` excluded); `composed`, `url` with the rows substituted under the identity resolver, each value percent-encoded with `postman-url-encoder` 3.0.8's `PATH_ENCODE_SET` (the C0 controls, DEL and every byte above it as UTF-8, space, `\"`, `<`, `>`, backtick, `#`, `?`, `{`, `}`; nothing else, so `/`, `%` and the rest of the reserved set pass) with a whole `{{token}}` kept verbatim. URLs are ASCII so an offset is the same number in both languages.",
